We now compare the results obtained for the bubble Reynolds number with a semiempirical correlation. By analogy to two-fluid wave theory, which yields an expression for 
the phase velocity of inviscid surface tension and gravity waves at a free surface, Mendelson 
[10] proposed the following formula for the terminal rise velocity of a bubble in the surfacetension or buoyancy force dominated regime

$$
U_{T}^{*}=\sqrt{\frac{2\sigma^{*}}{\rho_{l}^{*}d_{V}^{*}}+\frac{g^{*}d_{V}^{*}}{2}}.
$$

(7)

While Mendelson noted that the only justification for the above equation is how well it 
correlates experimental data, Marrucci et al. [9] extended the formula for liquid-liquid 
systems (i.e. a dispersed liquid drop rising in an immiscible continuous liquid) and proposed 
* * * *
2σ g d ∆ρ

$$
U_{T}^{*}=\sqrt{\frac{2\sigma^{*}}{\rho_{l}^{*}d_{V}^{*}}+\frac{g^{*}d_{V}^{*}\Delta\rho^{*}}{2\rho_{l}^{*}}}.
$$

(8)

Here, ∆*ρ<sup>*</sup>* is the difference between the densities of the continuous and dispersed phase. From 
Eq. (8) it is easy to show that the velocity becomes minimal when the equivalent diameter is

$$
\Delta\rho^{^*}
$$

---

****
exactly twice the Laplace length, i.e. for *dg* =Λ= 22 ( σ ∆ρ ). This condition 
V
corresponds to an Eötvös number of *EöB* = 4.

$$
d_{V}^{*}=2\Lambda=2\sqrt{\sigma^{*}/\left(g^{*}\Delta\rho^{*}\right)}
$$

$$
E\ddot{o}_{B}=4
$$

Recently, Tomiyama et al. [15] gave a physical interpretation why Eq. (8) correlates 
* * *
experimental data so well and noted that multiplying Eq. (8) by  ρ *d* µ yields 
*l* V l

$$
\rho_{l}^{*}d_{V}^{*}/\mu_{l}^{*}
$$

$$
R e_{B}=\left(2+\frac{1}{2}E\ddot{o}_{B}\right)^{0.5}\left(\frac{E\ddot{o}_{B}}{M}\right)^{0.25}.
$$

(9)

Thus, *ReB* is a function of *EöB* and *M* but does not depend on Γρ and Γµ. For the values *EöB* = 
-6
3.06 and *M* = 3.09·10 equation (9) gives *ReB* = 59.3. Thus, the value *Re* ≈ 56  obtained in 
B
